@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1002 = private unnamed_addr constant [1418 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWeak or broken cryptographic hash functions may be susceptible to\0Acollision attacks (where two different inputs produce the same hash) or\0Apre-image attacks (where an attacker can find an input that produces a\0Agiven hash). This can lead to security vulnerabilities in applications\0Athat rely on these hash functions.\0A\0AAvoid using weak or broken cryptographic hash functions in security\0Acontexts. Instead, use a known secure hash function such as SHA-256.\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule will also flag references to insecure hash functions.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives import hashes\0A\0Adigest = hashes.Hash(hashes.MD5())\0Adigest.update(b\22Hello, world!\22)\0Adigest.finalize()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives import hashes\0A\0Adigest = hashes.Hash(hashes.SHA256())\0Adigest.update(b\22Hello, world!\22)\0Adigest.finalize()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `hashlib` \E2\80\94 Secure hashes and message digests](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-327](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-328](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-916](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/916.html)\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
