@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1013 = private unnamed_addr constant [23 x i8] c"suspicious-mktemp-usage", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1014 = private unnamed_addr constant [1127 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `tempfile.mktemp`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`tempfile.mktemp` returns a pathname of a file that does not exist at the\0Atime the call is made; then, the caller is responsible for creating the\0Afile and subsequently using it. This is insecure because another process\0Acould create a file with the same name between the time the function\0Areturns and the time the caller creates the file.\0A\0A`tempfile.mktemp` is deprecated in favor of `tempfile.mkstemp` which\0Acreates the file when it is called. Consider using `tempfile.mkstemp`\0Ainstead, either directly or via a context manager such as\0A`tempfile.TemporaryFile`.\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule will also flag references to `tempfile.mktemp`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport tempfile\0A\0Atmp_file = tempfile.mktemp()\0Awith open(tmp_file, \22w\22) as file:\0A    file.write(\22Hello, world!\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport tempfile\0A\0Awith tempfile.TemporaryFile() as file:\0A    file.write(\22Hello, world!\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation:`mktemp`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/tempfile.html#tempfile.mktemp)\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1015 = private unnamed_addr constant [50 x i8] c"Use of insecure and deprecated function (`mktemp`)", align 1
