@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1069 = private unnamed_addr constant [43 x i8] c"abstract-base-class-without-abstract-method", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1070 = private unnamed_addr constant [1110 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for abstract classes without abstract methods or properties.\0AAnnotated but unassigned class variables are regarded as abstract.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAbstract base classes are used to define interfaces. If an abstract base\0Aclass has no abstract methods or properties, you may have forgotten\0Ato add an abstract method or property to the class,\0Aor omitted an `@abstractmethod` decorator.\0A\0AIf the class is _not_ meant to be used as an interface, consider removing\0Athe `ABC` base class from the class definition.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom abc import ABC\0Afrom typing import ClassVar\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(ABC):\0A    class_var: ClassVar[str] = \22assigned\22\0A\0A    def method(self):\0A        bar()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\0Afrom typing import ClassVar\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(ABC):\0A    class_var: ClassVar[str]  # unassigned\0A\0A    @abstractmethod\0A    def method(self):\0A        bar()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `abc`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/abc.html)\0A- [Python documentation: `typing.ClassVar`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.ClassVar)\0A", align 1
