@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1316 = private unnamed_addr constant [995 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for nested `min` and `max` calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ANested `min` and `max` calls can be flattened into a single call to improve\0Areadability.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aminimum = min(1, 2, min(3, 4, 5))\0Amaximum = max(1, 2, max(3, 4, 5))\0Adiff = maximum - minimum\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aminimum = min(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\0Amaximum = max(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\0Adiff = maximum - minimum\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis fix is always unsafe and may change the program's behavior for types without full\0Aequivalence relations, such as float comparisons involving `NaN`.\0A\0A```python\0Aprint(min(2.0, min(float(\22nan\22), 1.0)))  # before fix: 2.0\0Aprint(min(2.0, float(\22nan\22), 1.0))  # after fix: 1.0\0A\0Aprint(max(1.0, max(float(\22nan\22), 2.0)))  # before fix: 1.0\0Aprint(max(1.0, float(\22nan\22), 2.0))  # after fix: 2.0\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `min`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#min)\0A- [Python documentation: `max`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#max)\0A", align 1
