@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1419 = private unnamed_addr constant [2087 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `__all__` definitions that are not ordered\0Aaccording to an \22isort-style\22 sort.\0A\0AAn isort-style sort orders items first according to their casing:\0ASCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE names (conventionally used for global constants)\0Acome first, followed by CamelCase names (conventionally used for\0Aclasses), followed by anything else. Within each category,\0Aa [natural sort](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_sort_order)\0Ais used to order the elements.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AConsistency is good. Use a common convention for `__all__` to make your\0Acode more readable and idiomatic.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0A__all__ = [\0A    \22b\22,\0A    \22c\22,\0A    \22a\22,\0A]\0A\0Aif sys.platform == \22win32\22:\0A    __all__ += [\22z\22, \22y\22]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0A__all__ = [\0A    \22a\22,\0A    \22b\22,\0A    \22c\22,\0A]\0A\0Aif sys.platform == \22win32\22:\0A    __all__ += [\22y\22, \22z\22]\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe if there are any comments that take up\0Aa whole line by themselves inside the `__all__` definition, for example:\0A```py\0A__all__ = [\0A    # eggy things\0A    \22duck_eggs\22,\0A    \22chicken_eggs\22,\0A    # hammy things\0A    \22country_ham\22,\0A    \22parma_ham\22,\0A]\0A```\0A\0AThis is a common pattern used to delimit categories within a module's API,\0Abut it would be out of the scope of this rule to attempt to maintain these\0Acategories when alphabetically sorting the items of `__all__`.\0A\0AThe fix is also marked as unsafe if there are more than two `__all__` items\0Aon a single line and that line also has a trailing comment, since here it\0Ais impossible to accurately gauge which item the comment should be moved\0Awith when sorting `__all__`:\0A```py\0A__all__ = [\0A    \22a\22, \22c\22, \22e\22,  # a comment\0A    \22b\22, \22d\22, \22f\22,  # a second  comment\0A]\0A```\0A\0AOther than this, the rule's fix is marked as always being safe, in that\0Ait should very rarely alter the semantics of any Python code.\0AHowever, note that (although it's rare) the value of `__all__`\0Acould be read by code elsewhere that depends on the exact\0Aiteration order of the items in `__all__`, in which case this\0Arule's fix could theoretically cause breakage.\0A", align 1
